    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 4,
    "drivingExperience": "Painfully slow but undeniably charming in a utilitarian way. The 2.3L M111 engine is incredibly smooth and reliable, but asking it to move a 1.9-ton body-on-frame SUV is a monumental task. The ML 230 feels heavy, sluggish, and requires constant gear shifting using the slightly rubbery 5-speed manual to maintain highway speeds. It leans heavily in corners and lacks any sort of dynamic agility. However, if treated as a rugged, practical workhorse rather than a luxury performance vehicle, it offers an honest, durable, and surprisingly capable off-road driving experience.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The ML 230 is an interesting European anomaly. The M111 engine is a legendary tank; it features a heavy cast-iron block and duplex timing chain that will easily outlast the car it is bolted to. Mechanically, it is simple, cheap to repair, and incredibly forgiving of abuse.\n\nHowever, you must check for the dreaded 'oil in the wiring harness'. Pull the plug off the front camshaft magnet—if it is wet with oil, you must immediately install the 35 EUR isolation 'pigtail' harness, otherwise the oil will travel to the ECU and destroy it. Beyond the engine, the real risks lie in the early W163 chassis. Inspect the ladder frame meticulously for severe rust, check the transfer case for chain stretch (popping noises under load), and expect to replace the clutch frequently due to the massive weight of the vehicle. It is a highly reliable engine wrapped in a slightly problematic chassis, earning a very safe 4/10 risk rating.",
