    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 4,
    "drivingExperience": "The ML 350 was introduced during the W163's facelift, and the upgrade from 3.2L to 3.7L displacement makes a world of difference. The extra 35 Nm of torque significantly improves the driving dynamics of the heavy 2.1-ton SUV, meaning the 5G-Tronic doesn't have to downshift nearly as often on the highway. It pulls with a smooth, relentless, and refined wave of power. Visually and tactilely, the facelifted ML 350 is a vastly superior vehicle compared to the older ML 320; the interior plastics are significantly better, the climate controls are modernized, and the suspension feels slightly more planted, though it still exhibits the classic body-on-frame roll in corners.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The ML 350 represents the absolute peak of the W163 generation. By the time this 3.7L version was introduced in 2002, Mercedes had aggressively addressed the abysmal build quality and electrical gremlins that plagued the early 1997-2001 models. The AAM (All Activity Module) is vastly more reliable in the facelift, and the interior no longer rattles itself to pieces.\n\nMechanically, the 3.7L M112.970 is simply a bored and stroked version of the legendary 3.2L. This means it inherits the exact same bulletproof reliability and the exact same annoying oil leaks. You will absolutely have to change the valve cover gaskets, the oil filter housing seal, and the rear main seal eventually. The Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS) remains a hot-weather weak point, and the 722.6 transmission still requires the $10 13-pin connector replaced to prevent ATF wicking into the TCU. \n\nHowever, because this is a facelift model, you bypass many of the early W163 headaches. If you find a rust-free ML 350 (always check the rear frame rails and suspension mounts), fix the M112 oil leaks, and change the transmission fluid every 60k km, it is arguably one of the most reliable and capable family haulers Mercedes ever built. An easy 4/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "500 - 900 EUR / year",
