    "timingType": "Duplex timing chain. This is one of the most over-engineered timing systems Mercedes ever designed. If the engine receives regular oil changes with the correct synthetic fluid, this timing chain will easily outlast the vehicle's chassis, often surpassing 400,000 km without any measurable stretch.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-point manifold sequential fuel injection managed by the Bosch ME 2.0 system. It is a highly dependable, low-pressure system that avoids all the carbon buildup issues associated with modern direct injection engines.",
    "engineLifespan": "400,000 - 600,000+ km. The M113 V8 is globally recognized as one of the most durable and indestructible V8 engines ever manufactured by any automaker. The robust aluminum block, combined with Silitec cylinder liners and a massive oil capacity, ensures that mechanical failure of the rotating assembly is practically unheard of.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. Delivers a beautifully linear and predictable wave of torque perfectly suited for towing heavy loads or off-road driving.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME 2.0. A very stable and straightforward engine control unit, though it lacks the advanced OBD-II diagnostic depth of later ME 2.8 systems.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 2 / Euro 3",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "450+ hp. The internal components, including the forged connecting rods and robust crankshaft, are vastly over-engineered. This exact engine architecture was used as the base for the supercharged AMG M113K engines, proving its immense structural strength.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "18.0 - 20.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "12.0 - 14.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "14.5 - 16.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "22.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5). The exceptionally large 8-liter oil pan is the primary reason this engine is so durable; the oil is subjected to far less thermal breakdown over the service interval compared to smaller engines.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS) Failure: This is the most universally common issue on the M113 V8. As the engine bay gets hot, the internal resistance of the aging sensor fails, causing the engine to stall unexpectedly at traffic lights or refuse to start until the block completely cools down.",
      "Valve Cover Gasket Oil Leaks: The massive V8 engine generates significant heat, which bakes the rubber valve cover gaskets over time. They inevitably harden and leak oil directly onto the scorching hot exhaust manifolds, creating a highly noticeable burning oil smell inside the cabin through the HVAC system.",
      "Harmonic Balancer Rubber Separation: The crankshaft pulley consists of an inner and outer metal ring bonded by rubber. Over time and heat cycles, this rubber degrades and tears. If ignored, the outer ring separates, wobbles violently, and machines a massive hole directly through the timing chain cover and oil pan.",
      "PCV Breather Hose Degradation: The complex network of plastic and rubber crankcase ventilation hoses routed over the top of the valve covers becomes incredibly brittle from the V8 heat. They snap easily, leading to unmetered air entering the intake manifold, causing lean codes and rough idling.",
      "Oil Filter Housing Gasket Leak: The large gasket that seals the oil filter housing to the front of the engine block turns into hard plastic after a decade. Once it fails, it dumps a significant amount of oil down the front of the engine, often coating the serpentine belt and alternator.",
      "Mass Air Flow (MAF) Sensor Contamination: The Bosch MAF sensor is highly sensitive to dirt and oil vapor. A failing MAF will drastically reduce engine power, cause hesitation under heavy acceleration, and severely worsen the already high fuel consumption."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "722.6 Transmission 13-Pin Connector Oil Wicking: The electrical pilot bushing on the side of the 5G-Tronic transmission has O-rings that fail. Transmission fluid leaks past them and is drawn up through the wiring harness via capillary action, directly flooding and destroying the Transmission Control Unit (TCU) located in the passenger footwell.",
      "722.6 Conductor Plate Speed Sensor Failure: The plastic conductor plate located inside the transmission above the valve body houses vital speed sensors. When these sensors fail from age and metallic sludge, the transmission violently locks itself into 2nd gear (Limp Home Mode).",
      "BorgWarner Transfer Case Chain Stretch: The heavy ML430 relies on a chain-driven transfer case for its 4MATIC system. Pushing 390 Nm of torque through this chain over many years stretches it. Under heavy acceleration or towing, the loose chain slips over the gears, causing terrifyingly loud popping and banging noises from under the floor.",
      "All Activity Module (AAM) Electrical Gremlins: The pre-facelift W163 ML is notorious for having one of the worst body control modules in Mercedes history. The AAM routinely fails, causing bizarre issues like headlights turning on randomly, keys no longer starting the car, or the central locking completely dying.",
      "Engine and Transmission Mount Collapse: The sheer weight and low-end torque of the 4.3L V8 obliterate the fluid-filled rubber engine mounts. Once collapsed, the engine physically rests on the subframe, transmitting harsh V8 vibrations directly into the steering wheel and cabin.",
      "Severe Ladder Frame and Body Rust: Being a pre-facelift model manufactured in the early years of the Alabama plant, the ML430 suffers from horrific rust protection. The lower door seams, tailgate, and crucially, the rear sections of the ladder frame rust extensively, sometimes rendering the vehicle structurally unsafe."
    ],
