    "timingType": "Duplex timing chain. This is one of the most over-engineered timing systems Mercedes ever designed. If the engine receives regular oil changes with the correct synthetic fluid, this timing chain will easily outlast the vehicle's chassis, often surpassing 250,000 Miles without any measurable stretch.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-point manifold sequential fuel injection managed by the Bosch ME 2.8 system. Specifically tuned with aggressive catalyst heating routines to meet strict US ULEV (Ultra Low Emission Vehicle) standards.",
    "engineLifespan": "250,000 - 300,000+ Miles. The 5.0L M113 V8 is globally recognized as a pinnacle of durability. The robust aluminum block, combined with Silitec cylinder liners and a massive oil capacity, ensures that mechanical failure of the rotating assembly is practically unheard of.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. Delivers a beautifully linear and massive wave of 325 lb-ft torque, making it a completely effortless American highway cruiser and an outstanding towing vehicle.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME 2.8 with strict US OBD-II EVAP integration. Constantly monitors the fuel tank and emissions systems for vapor leaks.",
    "euroStandard": "US EPA Tier 2 / CARB ULEV",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "450+ hp. The internal components, including the forged connecting rods and robust crankshaft, are vastly over-engineered. This exact engine architecture was used as the base for the legendary supercharged AMG M113K engines.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "13 MPG",
      "highway": "17 MPG",
      "mixed": "15 MPG",
      "sporty": "10 MPG"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5 Quarts 0W-40 or 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5). The exceptionally large oil pan is the primary reason this engine is so durable; the oil is subjected to far less thermal breakdown over the service interval.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS) Failure: This is the most universally common issue on the M113 V8. As the engine bay gets hot, the internal resistance of the aging sensor fails, causing the engine to stall unexpectedly at traffic lights or refuse to start until the block completely cools down.",
      "Valve Cover Gasket Oil Leaks: The massive 5.0L V8 engine generates significant heat, which bakes the rubber valve cover gaskets over time. They inevitably harden and leak oil directly onto the scorching hot exhaust manifolds, creating a highly noticeable burning oil smell inside the cabin.",
      "Harmonic Balancer Rubber Separation: The crankshaft pulley consists of an inner and outer metal ring bonded by rubber. Over time and heat cycles, this rubber degrades and tears. If ignored, the outer ring separates, wobbles violently, and machines a massive hole directly through the timing chain cover.",
      "EVAP System Leaks (US Specific): Strict US emissions systems frequently trigger Check Engine Lights due to minute vapor leaks in the fuel cap seal, charcoal canister, or purge valve, which can prevent the vehicle from passing state emissions inspections.",
      "Oil Filter Housing Gasket Leak: The large gasket that seals the oil filter housing to the front of the engine block turns into hard plastic after a decade. Once it fails, it dumps a significant amount of oil down the front of the engine, often coating the serpentine belt and alternator.",
      "Ignition Coil Pack Degradation: Sitting directly on top of the extremely hot magnesium valve covers, the 8 dual-output ignition coil packs bake over time. They will eventually develop hairline cracks, leading to misfires under heavy load and a flashing Check Engine Light."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "722.6 Transmission 13-Pin Connector Oil Wicking: The electrical pilot bushing on the side of the 5G-Tronic transmission has O-rings that fail. Transmission fluid leaks past them and is drawn up through the wiring harness via capillary action, directly flooding and destroying the Transmission Control Unit (TCU).",
      "722.6 Conductor Plate Speed Sensor Failure: The plastic conductor plate located inside the transmission above the valve body houses vital speed sensors. When these sensors fail from age and metallic sludge, the transmission violently locks itself into 2nd gear (Limp Home Mode).",
      "BorgWarner Transfer Case Chain Stretch: The ML500 relies on a chain-driven transfer case for its 4MATIC AWD. Pushing a massive 325 lb-ft of torque through this chain over many years stretches it. Under heavy acceleration, the loose chain slips over the gears, causing terrifyingly loud popping noises.",
      "Severely Overworked Braking System: Despite having slightly larger brakes than the V6 models, the ML500 is simply too fast and too heavy (4,800+ lbs) for its braking system. Spirited driving will rapidly overheat the pads and warp the rotors, leading to severe steering wheel vibration when braking.",
      "Engine and Transmission Mount Collapse: The sheer weight and low-end torque of the 5.0L V8 obliterate the fluid-filled rubber engine mounts. Once collapsed, the engine physically rests on the subframe, transmitting harsh V8 vibrations directly into the steering wheel.",
      "Salt-Belt Ladder Frame Rust: In Northern US states and Canada, winter road salt absolutely devastates the W163's ladder frame, specifically around the rear suspension control arm mounts, sometimes rotting the steel completely through."
    ],
