    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 4,
    "drivingExperience": "The US-market ML 500 represents the pinnacle of non-AMG performance for the W163 generation in America. The upgraded 5.0L V8 delivers a massive 325 lb-ft of torque, making this heavy 4,800-lbs SUV feel incredibly light on its feet. It accelerates with a deep, refined V8 roar and pulls relentlessly on the highway, making overtaking and hauling family trailers completely effortless. Because it is a facelift model (2002-2005), the driving experience is vastly improved over the older ML 430; the interior plastics are much higher quality, the cabin is quieter, and the climate controls are modernized. However, the sheer speed of the 5.0L engine completely overwhelms the archaic ladder-frame chassis—it exhibits severe body roll in corners, the steering is vague, and the brakes feel terrifyingly inadequate when attempting to stop from high speeds.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The ML 500 is arguably the best overall package you can buy if you want a V8 W163 in North America. By the time this model was introduced during the facelift, Mercedes had ironed out the horrific build quality issues and electrical nightmares (like the failing AAM module) that ruined the reputation of the early 'Alabama Trash Can' models. \n\nMechanically, the 5.0L M113.965 engine is a legendary masterpiece of durability. If you respect the massive 8.5-Quart oil capacity, the engine block itself is immortal. However, it still suffers from the standard M113 annoyances: you will definitely be replacing the leaking valve cover gaskets, the hardened oil filter housing seal, and the Crankshaft Position Sensor. The harmonic balancer must be inspected regularly to prevent it from destroying the timing cover. \n\nThe real danger with the ML 500 is that the engine is too powerful for the drivetrain surrounding it. The 325 lb-ft of torque puts immense strain on the transfer case chain (which will stretch and pop) and destroys the rubber engine mounts rapidly. Furthermore, the 722.6 transmission is bulletproof, but only if you replace the $15 13-pin connector to prevent ATF from flooding the TCU. If you find a rust-free example and address the oil leaks, the ML 500 is a tremendously reliable and powerful daily driver. 4/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "$800 - $1,500 / year",
