    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 5,
    "drivingExperience": "The ML 270 CDI is the quintessential European workhorse. The 2.7L inline-5 engine has a distinct, unmistakable diesel clatter on cold startup, settling into a rhythmic, surprisingly smooth hum once warm. With 370 Nm of torque, it doesn't launch off the line, but it pulls with a stubborn, tractor-like persistence. The 5G-Tronic transmission shifts smoothly, optimizing the narrow diesel powerband. The heavy body-on-frame chassis means it leans heavily in corners and the steering is vague, but it thrives as an unstoppable towing rig or a long-distance highway cruiser. It feels utilitarian, incredibly rugged, and remarkably efficient for its size.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The ML 270 CDI with the OM612 engine is widely considered the best, most practical powertrain for the European W163. The engine block and rotating assembly are practically immortal, regularly clearing 500,000 km in taxi and fleet service. However, it is an older diesel, meaning you must be hyper-vigilant regarding its specific quirks. \n\nYou MUST pull the engine cover at every oil change and check for 'Black Death' (tar-like carbon leaking from the injector seals). If you catch it early, it's a 10 EUR fix; if you ignore it, the cylinder head is ruined. The plastic swirl flaps inside the intake manifold wear out and WILL eventually break, destroying the engine—deleting them with a kit is highly recommended. The thermostat will inevitably fail open (causing poor heating and fuel economy), and the Bosch CP1 fuel pump may leak diesel in cold weather.\n\nBeyond the engine, the W163 chassis remains the weak point. Rust on the ladder frame is a massive concern, and the BorgWarner transfer case chain is prone to stretching and popping under the heavy diesel torque. If you fix the swirl flaps, reseal the injectors, and rust-proof the frame, this SUV will easily outlive you. A solid 5/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "600 - 1,200 EUR / year",
