    "engineLifespan": "500,000+ km. The OM612 inline-5 block is legendary for its raw durability. The manual transmission itself is highly robust, though the Dual Mass Flywheel (DMF) is a known wear item due to the heavy chassis and diesel vibrations.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VNT). Controlled via vacuum to minimize turbo lag and optimize low-end diesel torque.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC15C6. For this manual transmission variant, the factory software utilizes a slightly modified torque curve (capping at 360 Nm instead of 370 Nm) delivered over a broader RPM range. This specific calibration was designed to protect the manual clutch and dual-mass flywheel from premature destruction.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 3",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220+ hp. The internal cast iron block and forged connecting rods can handle significantly more boost, though tuning a manual ML270 will rapidly destroy the dual-mass flywheel.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "10.5 - 12.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.0 - 8.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "9.0 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "12.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "7.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5). Crucial for protecting the turbocharger bearings and the simplex timing chain.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Dual Mass Flywheel (DMF) Rattle: The heavy diesel vibrations of the 5-cylinder engine, combined with the extreme weight of the SUV, tear the springs inside the DMF apart. This causes a loud clattering noise at idle and a shuddering clutch pedal.",
      "Injector Seal Failure ('Black Death'): The copper crush washers sealing the injectors to the cylinder head frequently fail. Exhaust gases escape, baking diesel fuel and soot into a hard, black, tar-like substance.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flaps: The plastic swirl flaps inside the intake manifold wear out at the pivot points. They leak thick, black boost pressure oil down the side of the engine block, and in severe cases, the flaps break off and are ingested by the engine.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (CP1) Leaks: The seals inside the Bosch CP1 high-pressure pump shrink and harden over time, causing raw diesel fuel to leak down the front of the engine.",
      "Thermostat Sticking Open: The OM612 thermostat frequently fails in the open position. The engine never reaches its 87°C operating temperature, leading to terrible fuel economy and accelerated engine wear.",
      "EGR Valve Clogging: The Exhaust Gas Recirculation valve becomes heavily caked in thick, oily soot. It sticks open or closed, throwing the vehicle into Limp Mode and completely killing turbo boost."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Clutch Burn-out Under Towing: Because the 2.7L engine lacks low-end grunt before the turbo spools, drivers often have to slip the clutch heavily to get the 2.1-ton SUV moving, especially when towing. This burns the friction disc rapidly.",
      "BorgWarner Transfer Case Chain Stretch: Pushing 360 Nm of heavy diesel torque through the chain-driven transfer case over many years causes the chain to stretch. Under heavy acceleration or towing, it slips over the gears, causing terrifyingly loud popping noises.",
      "Ladder Frame and Body Rust: Built during the lowest quality-control era of Mercedes, the ML270 CDI suffers from horrific rust protection. The lower door seams, tailgate, and crucially, the rear sections of the ladder frame rust extensively.",
      "Vacuum System Leaks: The VNT turbocharger and EGR valve rely entirely on vacuum lines. Hardened, cracked rubber vacuum lines lead to a total loss of turbo boost, throwing the engine into Limp Mode.",
      "All Activity Module (AAM) Electrical Gremlins: The central body control module is notorious for failing, causing bizarre issues like headlights turning on randomly or the central locking completely dying.",
      "Shifter Linkage Sloppiness: The manual gear selector uses plastic bushings that degrade over time, making finding gears (especially 1st and Reverse) incredibly vague and rubbery."
    ],
