    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 5,
    "drivingExperience": "The manual ML 270 CDI is an incredibly rare, utilitarian machine that feels more like driving a commercial truck than a luxury Mercedes. The 2.7L inline-5 engine has a distinct, off-beat diesel clatter. Because it has a manual transmission, you are completely at the mercy of the diesel's narrow powerband. Below 1,800 RPM, it suffers from significant turbo lag, forcing you to slip the clutch to get the heavy 2.1-ton SUV moving. However, once the VNT turbo spools, a wave of 360 Nm of torque hits, and the truck pulls with a stubborn, agricultural persistence. The 6-speed shifter is notoriously rubbery, vague, and requires a firm hand. It is not sporty or refined, but it feels rugged, highly engaging, and entirely unstoppable off-road.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The manual version of the ML 270 CDI eliminates the complex 5G-Tronic automatic transmission, replacing it with a simple 6-speed gearbox, but this introduces its own specific set of expensive problems. The OM612 engine itself is practically immortal, regularly clearing 500,000 km. However, the heavy diesel vibrations of the inline-5, combined with the extreme weight of the W163 chassis, puts a terrifying strain on the Dual Mass Flywheel (DMF). If the DMF starts rattling at idle, you are looking at a very expensive, labor-intensive replacement of the entire clutch assembly.\n\nEngine-wise, you MUST pull the plastic cover and check for 'Black Death' (tar-like carbon leaking from the injector seals). If you ignore it, the cylinder head is ruined. The plastic swirl flaps inside the intake manifold wear out and WILL eventually break, destroying the engine—deleting them with a kit is highly recommended. \n\nBeyond the powertrain, the W163 chassis remains the weak point. Rust on the ladder frame is a massive concern, and the BorgWarner transfer case chain is prone to stretching. If you delete the swirl flaps, reseal the injectors, rust-proof the frame, and accept that you will eventually buy a very expensive DMF clutch kit, this SUV is a tremendously rugged, manual-shifting survivor. A solid 5/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "600 - 1,200 EUR / year",
