    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. This is the Achilles heel of the OM628. The massive V8 diesel torque aggressively stretches the painfully thin simplex chain. When it stretches, the engine loses timing, rattles loudly, and will eventually jump teeth, destroying the engine. Replacing it requires a devastatingly expensive engine-out service.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch Common Rail Direct Injection (CDI). Features two high-pressure CP3 pumps and 8 individual solenoid injectors. Highly complex and prone to catastrophic 'Black Death' leaks on all 8 cylinders.",
    "engineLifespan": "150,000 - 250,000 km. Widely regarded by mechanics as one of the most problematic, fragile, and financially ruinous engines Mercedes-Benz ever produced. Cramming a twin-turbo V8 diesel into the W163 bay resulted in extreme heat issues and nearly impossible maintenance access.",
    "turboType": "Twin Variable Geometry Turbochargers (Biturbo VNT). Positioned on either side of the block, they provide a massive 560 Nm of torque, but are extremely difficult to access when their vacuum actuators fail.",
    "ecuType": "Dual Bosch EDC15C6 modules operating in a master/slave configuration. Electronically caps peak torque at exactly 560 Nm because the 5G-Tronic transmission and transfer case would literally shatter if exposed to the engine's true output.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 3",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "300+ hp. The internal block is strong, but tuning an OM628 is considered mechanical suicide. Raising the boost pressure rapidly accelerates the stretching of the timing chain and blows the head gaskets.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "14.5 - 16.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "9.5 - 11.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "11.5 - 13.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "18.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "10.0L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5). The massive 10-liter capacity is necessary to try and cool the immensely hot V8 diesel block.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Stretch: The simplex timing chain is too weak for the V8's torque. It stretches, causing rattling, poor starting, and eventually engine destruction. Replacement requires pulling the entire engine out of the vehicle.",
      "V8 'Black Death' (Injector Seal Failure): The OM628 has 8 injectors. The copper seals fail, leaking exhaust gas and diesel, which bakes into a rock-hard black tar over the top of the engine. Because it's a V8, rear injector access is virtually impossible without engine removal.",
      "Dual EGR Valve Clogging: The engine features two EGR valves that rapidly clog with oily soot from city driving. This throws the complex twin-turbo system into Limp Mode.",
      "Water-Cooled Alternator Failure: The 150A alternator is buried deep in the block and uses engine coolant to stay alive. When the internal seals fail, it leaks coolant or simply burns out, requiring a massive teardown to replace.",
      "Twin VNT Turbo Actuator Seizing: The variable vanes inside the twin turbochargers carbon up and seize. Because space is so tight, getting to the turbos to clean or replace them is a nightmare.",
      "High-Pressure CP3 Pump Leaks: The engine uses incredibly high fuel pressure. The seals on the Bosch CP3 pump degrade from heat, leaking raw diesel down the front of the V8 block."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "BorgWarner Transfer Case Explosion: The ML400 CDI produces 560 Nm of torque at incredibly low RPMs. This sheer rotational violence stretches the transfer case chain rapidly. Under hard acceleration, the chain will skip, bang, and eventually shatter the transfer case casing.",
      "722.6 Transmission Destruction: Even though it is the strongest version of the 5G-Tronic, hauling a 2.3-ton SUV with 560 Nm of diesel torque burns out the torque converter lock-up clutch and tears the internal planetary gears apart over time.",
      "Extreme Engine Bay Heat Degradation: The W163 engine bay was not designed for a Biturbo V8 diesel. The extreme heat bakes every piece of plastic, rubber vacuum line, and wiring harness brittle, leading to endless, untraceable electrical and vacuum faults.",
      "Impossible Maintenance Access: Simply changing the glow plugs or rear injectors requires unbolting the engine mounts and lowering the subframe. Labor costs for even basic repairs are astronomical.",
      "Front Suspension Collapse: The OM628 V8 is incredibly heavy. The torsion-bar front suspension of the W163 sags, wearing out ball joints, tie rods, and shock absorbers at an alarming rate.",
      "Ladder Frame Rust: Like all W163s, the ladder frame is highly susceptible to structural rust, particularly around the rear suspension mounts."
    ],
