    "riskRating": 5,
    "drivingExperience": "The W164 ML 350 represents a massive generational leap forward in refinement and driving dynamics compared to the older W163. The M272 3.5L V6 is silky smooth, revs eagerly to 6500 RPM, and produces a refined exhaust note that is vastly more sophisticated than the old 3-valve M112. Paired with the 7-speed 7G-Tronic automatic, power delivery is seamless, keeping the engine in its optimal torque band during highway passing. The unibody chassis (replacing the old truck-like ladder frame) makes the W164 feel planted, responsive, and composed through corners, virtually eliminating the terrifying body roll of its predecessor while maintaining incredible long-distance cruiser comfort.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The W164 ML 350 is a brilliant vehicle to drive, but owning an early model requires deep mechanical awareness. The absolute dealbreaker on pre-2007 M272 engines is the infamous **balance shaft sprocket wear issue**. If the engine serial number falls within the defective range and has not been repaired, the timing chain will eventually jump teeth and destroy the engine—a repair bill exceeding the car's market value. Always verify engine serial numbers or look for post-2007 facelifted models where Mercedes resolved the metallurgy.\n\nBeyond the balance shaft, the M272 has two guaranteed mechanical headaches: the plastic intake manifold runner arms *will* snap, and the oil cooler seals in the engine V *will* leak oil and coolant. Furthermore, the W164's integrated front differential is prone to bearing wear, which generates an expensive howling sound. If you buy a post-2007 model (or one with documented balance shaft repair), replace the intake arms with billet aluminum, and service the 7G-Tronic transmission every 60k km, it is a remarkably refined and durable luxury SUV. It earns a balanced 5/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "800 - 1,500 EUR / year",
