    "author": "Mercedes ML (https://skfb.ly/ox7RK) by [CRAKKER] is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
    "riskRating": 5,
    "drivingExperience": "The early W164 ML 500 offers a fascinating blend of old-school V8 muscle and modern unibody chassis dynamics. By carrying over the proven 5.0L M113 V8 from the W163 generation and mating it to the brand new 7-speed 7G-Tronic transmission, it delivers an effortless, incredibly smooth driving experience. The 460 Nm of torque comes on low in the rev range, making highway passing and heavy towing completely relaxed. The unibody chassis vastly improves cornering stability and ride comfort over the previous generation, especially when equipped with the AIRMATIC air suspension, which glides over road imperfections beautifully.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The 2005-2007 ML 500 represents a unique transitional period. Mechanically, the engine is an absolute tank. The M113 5.0L V8 is legendary for its durability, completely avoiding the catastrophic balance shaft and tumble flap issues that plague the newer M272/M273 engines. If you replace the harmonic balancer, reseal the valve covers, and keep a spare Crankshaft Position Sensor, the engine block will never let you down.\n\nHowever, the surrounding W164 technology is heavily flawed in these early years. The first generation of the 722.9 7G-Tronic transmission is notorious for burning out its VGS conductor plate, locking the vehicle in limp mode. Furthermore, water ingress through the taillights frequently destroys the Rear SAM electrical module, causing nightmarish electrical gremlins. The AIRMATIC suspension and the integrated front differential bearings are also guaranteed failure points. It is a bulletproof engine trapped in a highly temperamental early-production chassis. 5/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 1,800 EUR / year",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "Harmonic Balancer (M113)",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": -0.04,
        "z": 0.38,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Located at the front lower timing case. The rubber isolation ring degrades and tears. If ignored, it separates and can machine a hole straight through the engine block."
      },
      {
        "label": "Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS)",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.06,
        "z": 0.34,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Situated at the top rear of the engine block near the firewall. Bakes in V8 heat, fails frequently, and causes the engine to stall when hot."
      },
      {
        "label": "Valve Cover Gaskets & Ignition Coils",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.04,
        "z": 0.28,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Positioned over the cylinder banks. Rubber seals harden and shrink over time, causing severe oil leaks onto the glowing hot exhaust manifolds."
      },
      {
        "label": "7G-Tronic (722.9) Early VGS Unit",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": -0.12,
        "z": -0.08,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Integrated electro-hydraulic valve body and control unit (VGS) located underneath. Early versions suffer notorious speed sensor failures, locking the transmission into limp mode."
      },
      {
        "label": "Integrated Front Differential Housing",
        "x": -0.15,
        "y": -0.13,
        "z": 0.25,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Integrated directly into the transmission housing near the front axle. Internal needle bearings wear out, generating an expensive howling whirring noise that increases with vehicle speed."
      },
      {
        "label": "Front Lower Control Arm Bushings",
        "x": -0.2,
        "y": -0.11,
        "z": 0.32,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Located at the front wheel suspension assembly zone. Heavy rubber bushings crack and wear out, causing clunking noises over bumps and uneven front tire wear."
      }
    ]
