    "author": "Mercedes ML (https://skfb.ly/ox7RK) by [CRAKKER] is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "The ML 280 CDI offers a very relaxed, unhurried driving experience. The 190 hp output struggles slightly against the W164's 2.2-ton curb weight, resulting in sluggish acceleration off the line. However, the 440 Nm of diesel torque means that once it gets moving, it cruises effortlessly on the highway. The OM642 V6 is remarkably smooth and quiet for a diesel, lacking the agricultural clatter of older engines. Coupled with the 7G-Tronic transmission and unibody chassis, it feels planted and luxurious, though overtaking at high speeds requires patience.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The ML 280 CDI is a brilliant vehicle hiding a few catastrophic engineering oversights. The engine block itself is indestructible, but the OM642 is infamous for the 'V-Valley Oil Leak'. The orange oil cooler seals degrade, and fixing them requires a massive teardown. Furthermore, the turbo inlet seal drips oil straight onto the swirl flap (EKAS) motor, destroying it. If you buy this car, immediately upgrade the turbo seal and install a catch can. \n\nBecause it is software-detuned, the 280 CDI is arguably the best OM642 to tune—a Stage 1 remap safely unlocks the power it was originally designed to have (matching the 320 CDI). Beware of the early 7G-Tronic transmission failures (conductor plate) and potential AIRMATIC air suspension leaks. If the V-valley leak has been fixed with purple Viton seals and the transmission serviced, it is a highly reliable luxury cruiser. 6/10 Risk Rating due to high labor costs for seal repairs.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 1,800 EUR / year",
