    "author": "Mercedes ML (https://skfb.ly/ox7RK) by [CRAKKER] is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "The late-model W164 ML 300 CDI BlueEFFICIENCY offers an immensely refined, relaxed driving experience. The 204 hp output means it is not a performance vehicle, but the robust 500 Nm of torque delivered almost off idle allows the heavy 2.2-ton SUV to surge forward effortlessly in urban traffic and cruise silently on the highway. Thanks to the updated piezoelectric injectors and BlueEFFICIENCY sound deadening, the traditional diesel clatter is almost completely isolated from the luxurious cabin. The 7G-Tronic Plus transmission shifts seamlessly, maintaining optimal RPMs for fuel efficiency. It feels incredibly planted, safe, and luxurious, though overtaking at high highway speeds requires a firm, calculated press of the throttle.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The facelift ML 300 CDI represents the most refined version of the OM642 in the W164 chassis. Mercedes ironed out many early electrical bugs, but the fundamental engineering flaws of the V6 diesel remain. The engine block itself is indestructible, but the OM642 is globally infamous for the 'V-Valley Oil Leak'. The factory oil cooler seals degrade, and fixing them requires a massive teardown. Furthermore, the turbo inlet seal drips oil straight onto the swirl flap (EKAS) motor beneath it, causing catastrophic Limp Mode. If you buy this car, you MUST immediately upgrade the turbo seal and install a catch can.\n\nBecause it is software-detuned, the 300 CDI is arguably the smartest OM642 to tune—a Stage 1 remap safely unlocks the power it was originally designed to have, transforming the vehicle. Beware of potential AIRMATIC air suspension leaks and DPF clogging if used primarily for short city trips. If the V-valley leak has been fixed with purple Viton seals and the transmission serviced, it is a highly reliable luxury cruiser that will easily surpass 400,000 km. 6/10 Risk Rating strictly due to the massive labor costs associated with the inevitable oil cooler seal replacement.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 1,800 EUR / year",
