    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain driving four overhead camshafts. The OM642 utilizes a surprisingly thin, single-row timing chain for a massive V6 diesel. If oil changes are neglected or stretched to 25,000+ km, the chain physically elongates. This throws the valve timing out of phase, producing a sharp, metallic slapping noise against the timing cover on cold starts. Replacing the stretched chain requires expensive, specialized riveting tools.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch Common Rail Direct Injection (CDI) operating at an intense 1600 Bar, featuring highly precise piezoelectric injectors. This pre-facelift model was available in various Euro 4 configurations, some strictly utilizing a Diesel Particulate Filter (DPF), while certain export markets received DPF-delete hardware directly from the factory, making them highly sought after by tuners.",
    "engineLifespan": "400,000 - 500,000+ km. The core aluminum block and forged rotating assembly of the OM642 are globally recognized as virtually indestructible, frequently serving as heavy-duty fleet engines. However, realizing this immense lifespan requires extreme vigilance regarding the engine's external seals (specifically the oil cooler and turbo inlet) to prevent sudden and catastrophic oil loss.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VNT) with electronic actuation, positioned directly atop the engine in the V-valley. The electronic actuator adjusts the turbine vanes instantaneously to spool the turbo at exceptionally low RPMs.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC16+. The ML 320 CDI represents the factory 'unrestricted' software map for the pre-facelift OM642. It provides the full 224 horsepower and 510 Nm of torque the engine was physically engineered to produce out of the box.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 4 (Available with or without DPF depending on market code)",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "320+ hp. The internal forged crankshaft, cast-iron cylinder liners, and heavy-duty connecting rods are incredibly strong. The engine easily accepts substantial boost increases without risking internal structural failure, limited only by exhaust gas temperatures.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "12.0 - 14.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "8.0 - 9.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.0 - 11.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "14.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.51 STRICTLY REQUIRED if equipped with DPF). Using traditional, high-ash oil will rapidly and permanently clog the microscopic honeycomb structure of the Diesel Particulate Filter.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Turbo Inlet Seal Degradation: The absolute Achilles heel of the OM642. The large orange/red rubber seal mating the plastic intake pipe to the turbocharger degrades from oil vapor. Once it loses its seal, oil drips straight down into the V-valley, directly pooling onto the electronic EKAS (swirl flap) actuator motor, short-circuiting it and causing severe Limp Mode.",
      "V-Valley Oil Cooler Seal Failure: Deep inside the V of the engine, beneath the turbocharger, sits a liquid-cooled oil cooler. The factory orange seals bake in the extreme heat, harden, and inevitably leak. This results in massive oil puddles running down the bellhousing. Replacing a cheap seal requires over 10 to 12 hours of agonizing labor.",
      "Exhaust Manifold Slag Ingestion: The complex, dual-wall stainless steel exhaust manifolds have an inner liner that degrades over time due to intense exhaust heat. The inner wall breaks apart, sending destructive metallic shrapnel directly into the spinning turbine wheel of the turbocharger.",
      "Glow Plug Relay Module Burnout: The OM642 utilizes a solid-state electronic glow plug control unit mounted near the front of the engine. The internal aluminum bonding wires melt from engine vibration and heat, causing hard cold starts and an illuminated glow plug light.",
      "Injector 'Black Death': If the copper crush washer sealing the piezoelectric injector to the cylinder head fails, raw 1600-bar combustion gases escape. This mixture of diesel fuel and exhaust soot bakes onto the top of the cylinder head, solidifying into a rock-hard, tar-like substance.",
      "EKAS Swirl Flap Seizing: The intake manifolds feature butterfly valves to optimize low-RPM air swirl. Soot from the EGR valve mixes with oil vapor, creating a thick, sticky carbon paste that jams these flaps and breaks the plastic linkage arms."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "7G-Tronic (722.9) Early VGS Conductor Plate: Pre-facelift W164 models (2005-2008) feature the fragile early generation of the electro-hydraulic control unit (VGS). Failure of the internal speed sensors locks the gearbox in a violent Limp Mode, requiring an expensive valve body rebuild and dealer SCN coding.",
      "Integrated Front Differential Bearing Howl: The W164 4MATIC front differential shares its casing with the transmission bellhousing. The internal needle bearings wear prematurely under the heavy diesel torque, leading to a prominent howling or whirring noise.",
      "AIRMATIC Air Suspension Leaks: The 2.2-ton weight of the ML places immense strain on the rubber air struts. They develop microscopic dry-rot cracks, causing the vehicle to sag overnight and forcing the air compressor to run continuously until it burns out.",
      "Rear SAM Water Ingress: The foam seals around the rear taillights degrade over time, allowing rainwater to channel directly into the right rear quarter panel, destroying the highly expensive Rear Signal Acquisition Module (SAM)."
    ],
