    "author": "Mercedes ML (https://skfb.ly/ox7RK) by [CRAKKER] is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "The ML 320 CDI is the quintessential, definitive engine for the W164 generation. It strikes a nearly perfect balance between power, refinement, and fuel economy. The 224 hp output combined with a massive 510 Nm of diesel torque means the 2.2-ton SUV surges forward with an effortless, locomotive-like wave of power. Once at highway speeds, it cruises silently, with the 7G-Tronic transmission keeping the OM642 exactly in its optimal powerband. Unlike older diesels, the V6 is incredibly smooth and devoid of agricultural vibration. Thanks to the unibody chassis, it feels planted, safe, and luxurious, offering an outstanding long-distance driving experience.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The pre-facelift ML 320 CDI is a brilliant vehicle hiding a few catastrophic engineering oversights. The engine block itself is completely indestructible, but the OM642 is globally infamous for the 'V-Valley Oil Leak'. The factory orange oil cooler seals degrade, and fixing them requires a massive teardown. Furthermore, the turbo inlet seal drips oil straight onto the swirl flap (EKAS) motor beneath it, causing catastrophic Limp Mode. If you buy this car, you MUST immediately upgrade the turbo seal and install an oil catch can.\n\nBeyond the engine, early W164 models (2005-2008) suffer heavily from electronics issues. The 7G-Tronic transmission is notorious for burning out its VGS conductor plate, and the Rear SAM module is frequently destroyed by water leaking through faulty taillight seals. If the V-valley leak has been fixed with purple Viton seals, the transmission serviced, and the taillights resealed, it is a highly reliable luxury cruiser that will easily surpass 400,000 km. 6/10 Risk Rating strictly due to the massive labor costs associated with the inevitable oil cooler seal replacement and early transmission electronics.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 1,800 EUR / year",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "Turbo Inlet Seal & EKAS Motor",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.06,
        "z": 0.34,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Located top center at the turbocharger. The factory seal leaks oil directly onto the swirl flap (EKAS) motor beneath it, causing catastrophic Limp Mode. Must be replaced at every oil change."
      },
      {
        "label": "V-Valley Oil Cooler Seals",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.04,
        "z": 0.28,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Buried deep in the engine V under the intake manifolds. The factory seals fail, causing massive oil leaks down the back of the block. Requires ~10 hours of agonizing labor to replace with purple Viton seals."
      },
      {
        "label": "Glow Plug Control Module",
        "x": 0.1,
        "y": 0.07,
        "z": 0.30,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Mounted near the front of the engine, exposed to heat and vibration. Frequently burns out its internal solid-state wiring, causing cold start issues and Check Engine lights."
      },
      {
        "label": "7G-Tronic (722.9) Early VGS Transmission Unit",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": -0.12,
        "z": -0.08,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Integrated electro-hydraulic valve body and control unit (VGS) located underneath. Pre-facelift units are highly sensitive to dirty fluid; internal speed sensor failures lock the 7-speed transmission into limp mode."
      },
      {
        "label": "Integrated Front Differential Housing",
        "x": -0.15,
        "y": -0.13,
        "z": 0.25,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Integrated directly into the transmission housing near the front axle. Internal needle bearings wear out under heavy diesel torque, generating an expensive howling whirring noise that increases with vehicle speed."
      },
      {
        "label": "Rear Taillight Seals (To protect Rear SAM)",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.10,
        "z": -0.40,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Located at the rear of the vehicle. Foam seals degrade, allowing rainwater to channel into the right rear quarter panel, completely destroying the expensive Rear SAM electronic module."
      }
    ]
