    "id": "om642940-ml320cdi-bluetec",
    "brand": "Mercedes-Benz",
    "model": "ML 320 BlueTEC",
    "generation": "W164 (2005-2011)",
    "engineCode": "OM 642.940",
    "profileId": "om642940-ml320cdi-bluetec",
    "displacement": "2,987 cc",
    "power": "211 hp (155 kW)",
    "torque": "540 Nm",
    "fuelType": "Diesel (BlueTEC with AdBlue)",
    "cylinders": 6,
    "valves": 24,
    "compression": "18.0:1",
    "yearRange": "2008-2009",
    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain driving four overhead camshafts. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. If oil changes are stretched to 25,000+ km, the chain physically elongates. This throws the valve timing out of phase, producing a sharp, metallic slapping noise against the timing cover on cold starts. Replacing the stretched chain requires expensive, specialized riveting tools.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch Common Rail Direct Injection (CDI) operating at 1600 Bar, featuring piezoelectric injectors. This BlueTEC version introduces a complex SCR (Selective Catalytic Reduction) system, injecting AdBlue fluid into the exhaust to neutralize Nitrogen Oxides (NOx).",
    "engineLifespan": "300,000 - 400,000+ km. While the core aluminum block and forged rotating assembly are virtually indestructible, the lifespan of the BlueTEC variant is heavily compromised by the extraordinarily fragile, expensive, and failure-prone emissions control systems (AdBlue heater, NOx sensors) and the infamous OM642 oil cooler leaks.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VNT) with electronic actuation, positioned directly atop the engine in the V-valley.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC16+ / EDC17 (Market dependent). The software is heavily modified for the BlueTEC system. It is deliberately detuned to 211 hp to protect the sensitive NOx catalytic converter from excessive exhaust gas temperatures, but torque is raised to 540 Nm at lower RPMs to maintain heavy-duty pulling power.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5 / US EPA Tier 2 Bin 5 (BlueTEC SCR System)",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "320+ hp. The internal forged crankshaft and heavy-duty connecting rods are incredibly strong. However, tuning a BlueTEC model is extremely difficult due to the restrictive DPF and SCR systems. Significant gains require completely bypassing (deleting) the emissions hardware, which is illegal for street use.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "12.5 - 14.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.5 - 12.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "15.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.51 STRICTLY REQUIRED). Using traditional, high-ash oil will rapidly and permanently clog the microscopic honeycomb structure of the DPF and destroy the SCR catalyst.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "AdBlue Heater Failure: The absolute nightmare of the BlueTEC system. The heating element inside the AdBlue tank (located in the rear) burns out. The vehicle will display a 'Starts Remaining' countdown and eventually refuse to start. The factory fix requires replacing the entire tank assembly for thousands of Euros.",
      "NOx Sensor Failure: The exhaust system utilizes two highly sensitive NOx sensors that frequently fail due to soot contamination. When they fail, the ECU triggers Limp Mode and the dreaded 'Starts Remaining' countdown.",
      "Turbo Inlet Seal Degradation: The large orange/red rubber seal mating the plastic intake pipe to the turbocharger degrades. Oil drips straight down into the V-valley, pooling onto the electronic EKAS (swirl flap) actuator motor, short-circuiting it and causing severe Limp Mode.",
      "V-Valley Oil Cooler Seal Failure: Deep inside the V of the engine sits a liquid-cooled oil cooler. The factory orange seals bake in the extreme heat, harden, and leak. This results in massive oil puddles running down the bellhousing. Replacing a cheap seal requires over 10 to 12 hours of agonizing labor.",
      "Injector 'Black Death': If the copper crush washer sealing the piezoelectric injector fails, raw 1600-bar combustion gases escape. This mixture of diesel fuel and exhaust soot bakes onto the top of the cylinder head, solidifying into a rock-hard, tar-like substance."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "BlueTEC System Countdown: Any fault in the SCR system (low fluid, bad heater, bad NOx sensor) triggers a hardcoded countdown in the ECU. Once it reaches zero, the vehicle is completely immobilized and cannot be started until the dealer resets the system.",
      "7G-Tronic (722.9) Early VGS Conductor Plate: Pre-facelift W164 models feature the fragile early generation of the electro-hydraulic control unit (VGS). Failure of the internal speed sensors locks the gearbox in a violent Limp Mode.",
      "Integrated Front Differential Bearing Howl: The W164 4MATIC front differential shares its casing with the transmission bellhousing. The internal needle bearings wear prematurely under the heavy diesel torque, leading to a prominent howling or whirring noise.",
      "AIRMATIC Air Suspension Leaks: The 2.2-ton weight of the ML places immense strain on the rubber air struts. They develop microscopic dry-rot cracks, causing the vehicle to sag overnight."
    ],
