    "author": "Mercedes ML (https://skfb.ly/ox7RK) by [CRAKKER] is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "The ML 350 CDI (2009-2010) is fundamentally the exact same vehicle as the preceding ML 320 CDI, simply operating under a new badge introduced by Mercedes marketing. As such, it strikes the same nearly perfect balance between power, refinement, and fuel economy. The 224 hp output combined with a massive 510 Nm of diesel torque means the 2.2-ton SUV surges forward with an effortless, locomotive-like wave of power. Once at highway speeds, it cruises silently, with the 7G-Tronic transmission keeping the OM642 exactly in its optimal powerband. It feels incredibly planted, safe, and luxurious, offering an outstanding long-distance driving experience.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "Do not let the '350' badge fool you—this is mechanically, electrically, and physically identical to the 224-horsepower ML 320 CDI that preceded it. The ML 350 CDI is a brilliant vehicle hiding a few catastrophic engineering oversights that you must be prepared to handle.\n\nThe core aluminum engine block and forged internals are virtually indestructible, capable of half a million kilometers with ease. However, the OM642 is globally infamous for the 'V-Valley Oil Leak'. The factory orange oil cooler seals inevitably degrade and leak oil down the bellhousing. The seals themselves cost 20 Euros, but reaching them requires completely dismantling the top of the engine, removing the turbocharger and intake manifolds—a job that easily racks up 10 to 12 hours of agonizing labor. \n\nFurthermore, the red turbo inlet seal drips oil straight onto the swirl flap (EKAS) motor beneath it. When that motor short-circuits, the car enters a violent Limp Mode. If you buy this car, you MUST immediately upgrade the turbo seal and install an oil catch can. Beyond the engine, you must be aware of the 7G-Tronic transmission's VGS conductor plate issues, and the Rear SAM module being frequently destroyed by water leaking through faulty taillight seals. If the V-valley leak has been fixed with purple Viton seals, the transmission serviced, and the taillights resealed, it is a highly reliable luxury cruiser. If these haven't been done, you are buying a ticking financial time bomb. 6/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 1,800 EUR / year",
