    "author": "Mercedes ML (https://skfb.ly/ox7RK) by [CRAKKER] is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
    "riskRating": 9,
    "drivingExperience": "The driving experience of the ML 420 CDI is intoxicating. It is an absolute brute. Stepping on the throttle unleashes 700 Nm of torque almost instantly, catapulting the 2.3-ton SUV forward with the relentless, unstressed force of a freight train. It does not rev high, but it doesn't need to—the twin-turbo V8 produces a deep, nautical thrum that makes the vehicle feel utterly unstoppable. Highway passing is completely effortless, and when towing heavy loads, the engine barely notices the extra weight. It is the closest thing to driving a luxury tank on the autobahn.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The ML 420 CDI is an engineering marvel that should only be purchased by a master mechanic with a vehicle lift, or someone with a truly bottomless bank account. The OM629 is an incredible powerhouse, but its implementation in the W164 chassis is a catastrophic packaging nightmare. The 75-degree V8 block is shoehorned so tightly into the engine bay that there is literally zero working clearance. If you need to replace a starter motor, an exhaust manifold, or either of the twin VNT turbochargers, the official Mercedes workshop manual instructs you to unbolt the entire front subframe and drop the engine out of the bottom of the vehicle. \n\nThe V8 suffers from the same 'V-Valley Oil Leak' as the V6 OM642, but fixing it on the OM629 takes almost double the time (15-20 hours) because you have to strip away twin intake manifolds, complex fuel rails, and twin EGR coolers just to reach the seal. Furthermore, the 8 piezoelectric injectors love to seize in the cylinder heads, and the twin EGR valves fail constantly. \n\nBeyond the engine, the drivetrain is screaming for mercy. The 700 Nm torque output is the absolute structural ceiling for the early 7G-Tronic transmission; the clutches burn out, and the early VGS conductor plates fail. The front differential bearings howl under the strain, and the tremendous weight of the iron-lined V8 rapidly destroys the AIRMATIC front struts. It is a glorious, thunderous machine when it works, but it is an absolute financial disaster to maintain. 9/10 Risk Rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,500 - 5,000+ EUR / year (Engine-out procedures are common)",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "V-Valley Oil Cooler Seals",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.04,
        "z": 0.28,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Buried deep in the engine V under the twin intake manifolds. The factory seals fail, causing massive oil leaks. Requires ~15-20 hours of agonizing labor to strip the V8 top end to replace with Viton seals."
      },
      {
        "label": "Twin Variable Geometry Turbochargers",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.08,
        "z": 0.40,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Mounted on either side of the block. The electronic actuators bake and fail. Because there is zero clearance in the W164 bay, replacing the turbos generally requires dropping the entire engine and subframe."
      },
      {
        "label": "Twin EGR Valves",
        "x": -0.1,
        "y": 0.09,
        "z": 0.35,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "The engine uses two EGR valves which constantly foul with sticky diesel soot. They jam open, choking the engine, causing black smoke and severe Limp Mode."
      },
      {
        "label": "7G-Tronic (722.9) Early VGS Transmission",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": -0.12,
        "z": -0.08,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Pushed to its absolute physical limits by the 700 Nm V8 torque. Internal clutch packs wear rapidly, and the early pre-facelift VGS conductor plate frequently fails, locking the gearbox."
      },
      {
        "label": "AIRMATIC Front Air Struts",
        "x": -0.2,
        "y": -0.11,
        "z": 0.32,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "The OM629 is incredibly heavy. This extreme weight over the front axle destroys the air suspension rubber bladders and lower control arm bushings twice as fast as V6 models."
      },
      {
        "label": "Bosch CP3 High-Pressure Fuel Pump",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.05,
        "z": 0.42,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Located at the front of the block. Develops internal seal leaks over time, dripping diesel fuel down the front of the engine and deteriorating the serpentine belt."
      }
    ]
