    "timingType": "Duplex timing chain. The V8 OM629 utilizes a highly robust gear-driven camshaft system paired with a heavy-duty timing chain for the primary drive. It is incredibly strong, but if the chain tensioners fail due to neglected oil maintenance, the chain will slap and eventually require a catastrophic engine-out procedure to replace.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch Common Rail Direct Injection (CDI) operating at 1600 Bar, featuring 8 highly precise piezoelectric injectors. Requires flawless fuel filtration to prevent catastrophic injector failure.",
    "engineLifespan": "300,000 - 400,000 km. The core aluminum block and forged internals are virtually indestructible, but the engine is surrounded by failure-prone ancillaries. The ultimate lifespan is usually dictated by the owner's willingness to pay the astronomical labor costs required to fix oil leaks, turbos, or EGR systems, as the engine is shoehorned so tightly into the W164 chassis.",
    "turboType": "Twin Variable Geometry Turbochargers (BiTurbo / VNT) with electronic actuation. These are mounted on the outside of the cylinder banks. They spool rapidly, delivering immense boost at very low RPMs, but replacing them often requires dropping the entire engine and front subframe.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC16+. The ML 450 CDI is a direct facelift rebadge of the ML 420 CDI. It features slightly updated OBD2 and emissions software routines, but the core engine mapping remains identical, strictly flattening the torque curve at 700 Nm to protect the 7G-Tronic transmission.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 4 (with Twin DPFs)",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "400+ hp. The internal forged crankshaft, cast-iron cylinder liners, and heavy-duty connecting rods are vastly over-engineered. The engine can easily produce 850+ Nm of torque, but doing so will instantly destroy the automatic transmission.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "14.5 - 16.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "9.5 - 11.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "11.5 - 13.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "17.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "9.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.51 STRICTLY REQUIRED). Using high-ash oil will permanently clog the dual Diesel Particulate Filters. The massive 9.5-liter sump capacity is essential for managing the extreme heat generated by the twin turbos.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "V-Valley Oil Cooler Seal Failure: The V8 features a liquid-cooled oil cooler buried deep in the engine V. The factory seals harden and leak massive amounts of oil. Replacing this 20 EUR seal on the OM629 requires stripping twin intake manifolds, twin EGR coolers, and complex high-pressure fuel lines—easily a 15 to 20-hour labor job.",
      "Twin EGR Valve Failures: The engine features two separate Exhaust Gas Recirculation valves. They clog with thick carbon soot, sticking open and causing severe power loss, black smoke, and Limp Mode.",
      "Piezoelectric Injector Seizing: The 8 injectors are prone to failure and 'Black Death' (copper seal leaks). Even worse, due to water pooling on top of the massive V8, the injectors frequently rust and seize inside the cylinder heads, snapping during removal.",
      "Turbo Actuator Failures: The electronic actuators on the twin turbos bake in the engine bay heat and fail. Mercedes does not sell the actuators separately; they require complete turbocharger replacement, which requires dropping the engine.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Leaks: The massive Bosch CP3 fuel pump located at the front of the engine develops internal seal leaks, dripping diesel fuel down the front of the block and destroying the serpentine belt."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "7G-Tronic (722.9) Torque Overload: Even though this is a facelift model with slight transmission upgrades, the 722.9 was never originally designed to handle continuous 700 Nm diesel loads. The massive low-end grunt wears out the internal clutch packs and torque converter lockup clutch at an accelerated rate.",
      "Catastrophic Packaging Constraints: The 75-degree V8 is far too large for the W164 engine bay. There is zero clearance against the firewall or chassis rails. Simple jobs like replacing a starter motor, exhaust manifold, or turbocharger mandate completely dropping the front subframe and engine out of the bottom of the vehicle.",
      "AIRMATIC Air Suspension Collapse: The OM629 is profoundly heavy. The sheer weight over the front axle destroys the rubber air struts, lower control arm bushings, and ball joints twice as fast as the gasoline V6 models.",
      "Integrated Front Differential Bearing Howl: The internal needle bearings in the front differential are pulverized by the 700 Nm of torque, leading to a prominent howling noise."
    ],
