        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GLS/gls.glb", model3DScale: 0.05, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.12, y: 0, z: 0.35 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights1", "headlights2"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: ".", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 100, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 28, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The GLS 450 is the definitive luxury family hauler for the North American market. The M276 3.0L V6 BiTurbo is incredibly smooth and quiet, offering an exceptionally refined experience. Thanks to the factory boost increase to 367 hp, it handles the massive 5,300-lb curb weight noticeably better than the European GLS 400. Mercedes tuned the ECU to spool the turbos very early, providing adequate, completely un-dramatic acceleration around town. It will never press you into your seat like the V8 models, but for effortless family hauling and interstate cruising, the power is perfectly sufficient and delivered with zero harshness.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic's perspective, the M276 3.0L BiTurbo in the GLS 450 is arguably the most reliable petrol engine available for the X166 chassis. Why? Because it completely avoids the catastrophic cylinder scoring issues of the M278 V8s found in the GLS 500/550, and crucially, it is a pure 12V combustion engine—meaning you entirely avoid the terrifying 48V mild-hybrid electrical failures of the newer generation.\n\nMechanically, the engine is brilliant, but it is under immense constant strain trying to drag 2.4 tons of weight. The turbos are spooled constantly to generate low-end torque. This creates massive under-hood heat that bakes the plastic turbo coolant lines—these WILL shatter, and you MUST proactively upgrade them to billet aluminum. Because of the direct injection, the heavy load causes more blow-by, leading to rapid intake valve carbon coking; budget for Walnut Blasting every 100,000 km. If you change the oil strictly every 10,000 km to protect the chain tensioners, fix the coolant lines, and clean the intake valves, this engine is a bulletproof luxury workhorse.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,000 - $2,000 / year (The lack of a 48V hybrid system or complex V8 twin-turbos makes this highly reasonable for a flagship SUV. However, you MUST budget for the inevitable Turbo Coolant Line replacement (400 EUR for billet upgrades) and Intake Valve Walnut Blasting (450 EUR) every 100k km. The massive weight means AIRMATIC air struts and compressors will fail prematurely (1,000+ EUR per axle). Strict 10,000 km oil changes (110 EUR) are mandatory to protect the timing chain.)",
