        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GLS/glsx167.glb", model3DScale: 0.12, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], "manualLightPositions": { "headlights": { x: 0.12, y: 0.01, z: 0.38 } }, "customLightNames": { "headlights": ["headlights1", "headlights2", "headlights3", "headlights4"], "taillights": ["taillights"], "drllight": ["drls1", "drls2", "drls3"] }, author: "Mersedes- Benz GLS (https://skfb.ly/oRnOv) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",  "lightSettings": { "headlightAngle": 0.25, "headlightIntensity": 100, "headlightOpacity": 0.5, "headlightRadiusTop": 0.02, "headlightTargetDistance": 4.5, "headlightPenumbra": 0.1, "meshGlowHeadlight": 10, "meshGlowTaillight": 14, "meshGlowDrl": 20, "useBloom": true },
        "hotspotSettings": { "distanceFactor": 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The X167 GLS 450 with the M256 inline-6 is a masterclass in modern luxury propulsion. Because inline-6 engines are inherently perfectly balanced, the engine is whisper-quiet and practically vibration-free at idle. What truly sets it apart is the 48V EQ Boost system: the electric auxiliary compressor (eZV) and the Integrated Starter Generator (ISG) work together to completely eliminate turbo lag. When you press the throttle from a standstill, the electric motor dumps 250 Nm of torque instantly into the drivetrain while the electric supercharger spools, launching the massive 2.5-ton SUV with effortless, linear authority before the twin-scroll turbo takes over.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M256 inline-6 in the X167 GLS 450 is a magnificent engineering achievement, but from an independent mechanic's standpoint, it represents a steep climb in diagnostic complexity compared to older 12V engines. Mechanically, the closed-deck aluminum block, NANOSLIDE cylinder walls, and inline-6 rotating assembly are virtually indestructible—completely eliminating the terrifying cylinder scoring issues of earlier M278 V8s.\n\nHowever, the peripherals and layout introduce significant financial and maintenance risks. First, the timing chain is mounted at the REAR of the engine against the firewall. If extended oil changes allow the chain to stretch, replacing it requires dropping the transmission or pulling the engine—an astronomical labor bill. Second, the entire front end is beltless; cooling is handled by a 48V electric water pump that can fail instantly without warning, risking a warped cylinder head. Finally, the 48V EQ Boost electrical system is the vehicle's Achilles' heel. If the 48V lithium battery, DC/DC converter, or ISG inverter fails out of warranty, the car is bricked ($3,000+ repair). Change the oil strictly every 10,000 km, ensure the dealer applies all 48V BMS software updates, and budget for intake walnut blasting every 100,000 km.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,500 - $3,000+ / year (While the core inline-6 engine is mechanically solid, the 48V EQ Boost system introduces major financial risk. If the 48V lithium battery or ISG fails out of warranty, repairs exceed $3,000. Replacing the 48V Electric Water Pump is a $900+ job. You MUST budget for Intake Valve Walnut Blasting (480 EUR) every 100k km. The massive 2.5-ton curb weight means AIRMATIC air struts and control arms will require frequent replacement ($1,200+ per axle). Strict 10,000 km oil changes (150 EUR) are mandatory to protect the rear-mounted timing chain.)",
