        riskRating: 8,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GLS/glsx167.glb", model3DScale: 0.12, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.12, y: 0.01, z: 0.38 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights1", "headlights2", "headlights3", "headlights4"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2", "drls3"] }, author: "Mersedes- Benz GLS (https://skfb.ly/oRnOv) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 10, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 20, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The X167 GLS 580 represents the pinnacle of civilized V8 luxury. The 4.0L Biturbo engine operates in near silence under light loads, seamlessly dropping to 4 cylinders to sip fuel. However, when you demand power, the 48V ISG instantly dumps 250 Nm of electric torque into the driveline, completely masking any turbo lag, before the V8 awakens with a muted, muscular roar. It launches the 2.6-ton mansion with shocking, linear violence. The ride remains utterly composed thanks to the massive air suspension, delivering a level of effortless passing power that completely dominates highway travel.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M176 V8 in the GLS 580 is a mechanical triumph that solves the terrifying cylinder scoring issues of the old M278, but it replaces them with an incredibly volatile 48V electrical nightmare. Mechanically, the block is strong, but the 'Hot-V' twin-turbo layout traps intense heat that bakes the plastic coolant lines until they shatter. Proactively upgrading to billet coolant lines is mandatory.\n\nThe true risk lies in the beltless front end and the 48V ISG. Because there is no serpentine belt, the engine relies entirely on a 48V electric water pump. If that pump fails, the engine overheats instantly. If the 48V lithium battery fails, the car is permanently bricked and cannot even be jump-started (a $3,000+ repair). Furthermore, the timing chain is located at the rear of the engine. If you stretch the oil change intervals and the tensioners fail, replacing the chain requires dropping the entire transmission and hybrid motor. Buy this SUV only if you have a comprehensive factory warranty and the financial fortitude to handle the 'S-Class tax' on all repairs.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,500 - $5,000+ / year (The M176 V8 combined with the 48V system carries extreme financial risk. If the 48V battery or DC/DC converter fails out of warranty, it is a $3,000+ repair. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line replacement (500 EUR for billet upgrades) and Intake Valve Walnut Blasting (600 EUR) every 100k km. The massive V8 weight means AIRMATIC air struts will fail prematurely (1,200+ EUR per axle). Strict 8,000 km oil changes (160 EUR) are mandatory to protect the rear timing chain and cylinder deactivation lifters.)",
