        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-Benz GLE 450 d (V167 Facelift)", note: "The slightly smaller SUV sibling utilized the exact same 367 hp OM 656 M hybrid diesel setup." }, { name: "Mercedes-Benz S 450 d (W223)", note: "The flagship sedan used this engine to provide long-range, hyper-efficient, effortless executive cruising with EQ Boost smoothness." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "400 - 420 hp (ICE)", notes: "MODERATE GAINS. The core OM656 block can handle huge power, but tuning the Facelift 450 d is heavily restricted by the 48V ISG integration. Aggressive mapping can cause severe juddering as the ECU struggles to blend the electric ISG torque with the elevated mechanical boost. Pushing torque past 900 Nm risks slipping the transmission clutches.", zeroToHundred: "5.5s", hundredToTwoHundred: "14.8s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (DPF/EGR Delete & Tune)", power: "440 - 460 hp", notes: "Requires physical removal of the close-coupled DPF, underfloor SCR, and blocking the EGR (FOR OFF-ROAD USE ONLY). Drops EGTs massively and solves intake carbon sludge issues. However, the complexity of tricking the 48V Hybrid ECU into accepting the deleted sensors makes this incredibly difficult and expensive.", zeroToHundred: "5.1s", hundredToTwoHundred: "13.5s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 367, nm: 750 }, stage1: { hp: 410, nm: 850 }, stage2: { hp: 450, nm: 920 }, stage3: { hp: 480, nm: 980 } },
        riskRating: 7,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GLS/glsx167.glb", model3DScale: 0.12, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.12, y: 0.01, z: 0.38 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights1", "headlights2", "headlights3", "headlights4"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2", "drls3"] }, author: "Mersedes- Benz GLS (https://skfb.ly/oRnOv) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 10, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 20, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The Facelift GLS 450 d represents the absolute pinnacle of diesel luxury. By marrying the mighty 2.9L inline-6 diesel with the 48V EQ Boost ISG system, Mercedes created a monster of efficiency and refinement. When you press the throttle, the 48V electric motor instantly dumps 200 Nm of torque into the transmission, completely obliterating any trace of turbo lag before the massive sequential mechanical turbos take over. It surges forward with a relentless 750 Nm wave of force, remaining eerily silent in the cabin. The hybrid system allows the engine to shut off completely at highway speeds, letting the 2.5-ton SUV 'sail' in total silence while returning fuel economy that borders on witchcraft.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From an engineering standpoint, the OM 656 M in the GLS 450 d is a technological marvel. From an independent mechanic's perspective, it is a terrifying marriage of diesel soot and fragile 48V electrical architecture. \n\nMechanically, the engine block is immortal (steel pistons in an aluminum block mean zero cylinder scoring). However, by adding the 48V EQ Boost system, you now have the compounded risks of two entirely different powertrains. If the 48V lithium battery fails out of warranty, the car is permanently 'bricked' ($3,000+ repair). Furthermore, to fit the ISG hybrid motor on the transmission bellhousing, the timing chain was crammed even tighter against the firewall. If you stretch oil intervals and that chain wears out, replacing it requires a massive engine/transmission drop.\n\nEven worse, the hybrid 'coasting' feature (which shuts the engine off while driving) actively prevents the exhaust from reaching the extreme temperatures required to regenerate the strict Euro 6e DPF filters. If driven in the city, the DPF will block rapidly. You absolutely must drive this car hard on the highway to keep it alive. Furthermore, check the oil pump solenoid connector immediately; if oil wicks up that harness, it will destroy the ECU. Strict 10,000 km oil changes, regular highway driving, and a trickle charger are mandatory.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,000 - $4,000 / year (The 450 d carries extreme financial risk due to the 48V Hybrid system. If the 48V lithium battery or ISG fails out of warranty, repairs exceed $3,000. It also suffers from traditional diesel issues: Intake Manifold Cleaning (600 EUR) and NOx sensor failures (500+ EUR) are common. The massive 2.5-ton weight means AIRMATIC air struts will fail prematurely (1,200+ EUR per axle). Strict 10,000 km oil changes (140 EUR) and 40,000 km Fuel Filters (90 EUR) are absolutely non-negotiable to protect the rear timing chain and HPFP.)",
